  "preamble": "Monophthongizing [ai] to [i] means the nucleus takes on the quality of the high front offglide. In Akkadian, the Proto-Semitic diphthong *ay contracted to ī (*bayt- 'house' > bītum, *ʕayn- 'eye' > īnum), and *aw contracted to ū in the same way. Moroccan and Algerian Arabic made the same change independently, in contrast to the [eː] of Egyptian and Levantine Arabic (Classical bayt > Moroccan bīt 'room', layl > līl 'night'). In western Austronesian, Proto-Malayo-Polynesian final *-ay gives -i in Malay and Old Javanese (*qatay 'liver' > hati, *pajay 'rice' > padi, pari), while Philippine languages keep -ay (Tagalog atay, palay).",
  "phoneticEffects": "Monophthongization, Raising",
